Draw the LAN waiting room and let the host start the game early.

chocolate-doom's net_gui.c shows a waiting room while a network game
gathers players: it lists who has joined, the host (the controller)
starts the game with Space whenever it chooses, and Esc quits. fbDOOM
does not link chocolate-doom's textscreen library, so 0007's
NET_WaitForLaunch replaced that room with a headless loop that could
only start a -nodes N game once all N players had joined, on a display
that stayed dark because the game opens /dev/fb0 after this wait.

Open the display early, draw the same room with Doom's own font, and
read Space and Esc from the raw-mode keyboard. Each change to the room
is also printed, as "N of M players have joined". -nodes N still starts
the game by itself, as upstream does. I_InitGraphics now ignores a
second call, since /dev/fb0 has a single owner.

Reference: chocolate-doom src/net_gui.c (OpenWaitDialog, StartGame,
EscapePressed) at the pinned commit.

